# Line endings. Sift is developed and shipped on Windows while CI runs on Linux and the
# published image is Debian, so the same files are read by both. Without this file git would apply
# its per-machine default and a first commit made on Windows could rewrite every source file,
# burying the real change in ~1,300 lines of noise and, worse, breaking the shell scripts, which a
# CRLF turns into a "bad interpreter" error that names the wrong problem.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` means: git decides what is text, and every text file is LF in the working
# tree AND in the repository, on every platform. Nothing is converted, so nothing drifts.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The two exceptions, and they are real. Windows batch files are read by cmd.exe, which needs CRLF
# for multi-line constructs; PowerShell is more tolerant but is kept consistent with them.
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# A patch the build applies is pinned by digest: its bytes are never converted on any platform.
*.patch  -text

# Binary. Marked explicitly rather than left to detection: a heuristic that guesses wrong on one
# file corrupts it silently, and one NUL byte is enough to make a source file read as binary.
# Fonts, media fixtures, compiled addons and archives never get touched.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.avif binary
*.ico  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.mov  binary
*.webm binary
*.mkv  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.onnx binary
*.node binary
*.dll  binary
*.exe  binary
*.pyd  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.7z   binary
